fn page_docs_functions() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"active\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Functions</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">Functions are declared with <code>fn</code>. Parameters are typed, and a return type is written after an arrow.</p> <h2>Parameters and return types</h2> <p>This program prints <code>5</code>. <code>print</code> takes only a <code>str</code> and adds no newline, so numbers go through <code>to_str</code>.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn add(a: int, b: int) -&gt; int {\n    return a + b;\n}\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(to_str(add(2, 3)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Returning early</h2> <p>Output: <code>negative</code></p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn classify(n: int) -&gt; str {\n    if n &gt; 0 {\n        return &quot;positive&quot;;\n    } else if n &lt; 0 {\n        return &quot;negative&quot;;\n    } else {\n        return &quot;zero&quot;;\n    }\n}\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(classify(0 - 4) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Recursion</h2> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn factorial(n: int) -&gt; int {\n    if n &lt;= 1 {\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(to_str(factorial(5)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <p>This program prints <code>120</code>. Programs end with <code>return main();</code> on the last line, and <code>main</code>'s return value becomes the exit status. A file containing only <code>fn main() {...}</code> prints nothing, because top-level statements are what run.</p> <h2>Procedures</h2> <p>A function without a return type performs work without producing a value. This one prints <code>Hello, RUDRA</code>.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn greet(name: str) {\n    print(&quot;Hello, &quot; + name + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n}\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    greet(&quot;RUDRA&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>   <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\">Previous: Variables and Types</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/control-flow\">Next: Control Flow</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_getting_started() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"active\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Getting Started</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">This guide takes you from install to a running program in a few minutes. Every command and example here was checked against the shipped RUDRA 1.1.0 toolchain.</p> <h2>1. Install the toolchain</h2> <p>Download the Linux x86_64 archive from <a href=\"/downloads/\">Downloads</a>, verify its checksum, then install it:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>tar -xzf rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz\ncd rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64 &amp;&amp; ./install.sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"</code></pre> </div>  <p>This installs <code>ruxc</code> and <code>rudra</code> into <code>~/.local/bin</code>. To check the install works, compile and run the bundled example in the next steps (<code>share/rudra/examples/hello.rux</code> in the archive).</p> <h2>2. Your first program</h2> <p>Create a file named <code>hello.rux</code>. A runnable program defines <code>main</code> and ends with <code>return main();</code>:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(&quot;Hello, RUDRA!\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>3. Compile and run</h2> <p>Use the compiler directly:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>ruxc hello.rux hello &amp;&amp; ./hello</code></pre> </div>  <p>This prints <code>Hello, RUDRA!</code>. The <code>rudra</code> wrapper takes the same arguments (<code>rudra hello.rux hello</code>) and also makes the std library importable, because <code>ruxc</code> resolves imports only from the source file's own directory.</p> <p>The 1.1.0 release has no project scaffolding: there is no <code>rudra new</code>, <code>rudra run</code>, <code>Rux.toml</code> or <code>--run</code> flag. A driver with <code>build</code>, <code>run</code> and <code>check</code> commands exists in the source repository but is not yet released.</p> <div class=\"callout callout-info\"> <h4>Next</h4> <p>Continue to <a href=\"/docs/installation/\">Installation</a> for platform details, or jump to <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals/\">Language Fundamentals</a>.</p> </div>  <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <span></span> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/installation\">Next: Installation</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_installation() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"active\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Installation</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">The RUDRA toolchain ships as downloadable release assets. The current 1.1.0 release is the self-hosted toolchain and targets Linux x86-64 only. The earlier 1.0.0 and 1.0.1 releases were a different, Rust-based toolchain and are not current.</p> <h2>Available builds</h2> <table> <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Asset</th><th>Release</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Linux (x86-64)</td><td><code>rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz</code></td><td>v1.1.0</td></tr><tr><td>Debian / Ubuntu (amd64)</td><td><code>rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-amd64.deb</code></td><td>v1.1.0</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>macOS and Windows are not supported by 1.1.0.</p> <h2>Linux (x86-64) tarball</h2> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>tar -xzf rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz\ncd rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64 &amp;&amp; ./install.sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"</code></pre> </div>  <p>The archive contains <code>bin/ruxc</code> (the compiler), <code>bin/rudra</code> (a POSIX shell wrapper), <code>install.sh</code>, <code>uninstall.sh</code>, the 68-module std library under <code>lib/rudra/std</code>, <code>lib/rudra/compiler/tokens.rux</code> and <code>share/rudra/examples/hello.rux</code>. The installer places <code>ruxc</code> and <code>rudra</code> under <code>~/.local</code> by default. Ensure <code>~/.local/bin</code> is on your PATH.</p> <h2>Debian / Ubuntu package</h2> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>sudo dpkg -i rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-amd64.deb</code></pre> </div>  <p>The package installs <code>/usr/bin/ruxc</code>, <code>/usr/bin/rudra</code> and the std library in <code>/usr/lib/rudra/std</code>.</p> <h2>Check the install</h2> <p>There is no <code>version</code> subcommand in this release. To confirm the install works, compile and run the bundled example:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>ruxc share/rudra/examples/hello.rux hello &amp;&amp; ./hello</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Verify your download</h2> <p>Always confirm the SHA256 checksum shown on the <a href=\"/downloads/\">Downloads</a> page before installing:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>sha256sum rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz</code></pre> </div>  <div class=\"callout callout-info\"> <h4>About the binaries</h4> <p><code>ruxc</code> is a self-hosted compiler written in RUDRA. It is a static ELF with zero runtime dependencies, and it emits static ELF executables directly. The 1.1.0 release does not include <code>rudrac</code>, <code>ruxpkg</code> or <code>rudra-lsp</code>. See <a href=\"/transparency\">Transparency</a> for details.</p> </div>  <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/getting-started\">Previous: Getting Started</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\">Next: Language Fundamentals</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_language_fundamentals() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"active\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Language Fundamentals</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">A RUDRA program is a set of functions. A file's top-level statements run in order, and the conventional last line is <code>return main();</code>.</p> <h2>Program structure</h2> <p>A file containing only <code>fn main() {...}</code> prints nothing. End the file with <code>return main();</code>; the exit status is the value <code>main</code> returns. This program prints <code>Hello, RUDRA!</code>.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(&quot;Hello, RUDRA!\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Statements</h2> <p>Statements end with a semicolon. Bindings, assignments, function calls and control-flow blocks make up the body of a function.</p> <h2>Comments</h2> <p>RUDRA supports line comments that start with <code>//</code>. Block comments (<code>/* ... */</code>) are not supported. This documentation keeps its samples comment-free.</p> <h2>Printing</h2> <p>The built-in <code>print</code> takes only a <code>str</code> and adds no newline. Convert other values first: <code>to_str</code> for integers, and <code>bool_to_str</code> (module <code>convert</code>) and <code>fmt_f64</code> (module <code>fmath</code>) for booleans and floats. Programs that import std modules are compiled with <code>rudra</code>. This program prints <code>text</code>, <code>42</code>, <code>3.14</code> and <code>true</code>, each on its own line.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>import &quot;fmath&quot;;\nimport &quot;convert&quot;;\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(&quot;text\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(42) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(fmt_f64(3.14, 2) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(bool_to_str(true) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>   <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/installation\">Previous: Installation</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\">Next: Variables and Types</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_modules() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"active\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Modules and Projects</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">RUDRA 1.1.0 organises code as files that import each other with <code>import \"name\";</code>. There are no project manifests or scaffolding commands in this release.</p> <h2>Writing a module</h2> <p>Any <code>.rux</code> file is a module. Put functions in it and leave out the <code>return main();</code> line. Save this as <code>shapes.rux</code>:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn square(n: int) -&gt; int {\n    return n * n;\n}\nfn cube(n: int) -&gt; int {\n    return n * square(n);\n}</code></pre> </div> <h2>Importing a module</h2> <p>A file next to it can start with <code>import \"shapes\";</code> and call <code>cube(3)</code>; compiled with <code>ruxc main.rux main</code>, it prints <code>27</code>. <code>ruxc</code> resolves imports only from the directory of the source file being compiled, so keep the modules beside it.</p> <p>The std library is imported the same way. Because it is not in your source directory, compile such programs with <code>rudra</code>, which copies the std library next to a temporary copy of the source first:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>import &quot;math&quot;;\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(to_str(gcd(12, 18)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>rudra gcd.rux gcd &amp;&amp; ./gcd</code></pre> </div> <p>This prints <code>6</code>.</p> <h2>Run and build</h2> <p>There is no <code>rudra new</code>, <code>rudra run</code>, <code>rudra build</code>, <code>rudra check</code> or <code>Rux.toml</code> in the released toolchain. Compile with <code>ruxc file.rux output</code> (or <code>rudra file.rux output</code>) and run <code>./output</code>.</p> <div class=\"callout callout-info\"> <h4>Not yet released</h4> <p>The source repository contains a newer driver (<code>driver/rudra.rux</code>) with <code>build</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>check</code> and <code>version</code> commands, and a package tool (<code>package-manager/ruxpkg.rux</code>) that verifies a manifest module's SHA-256. Neither is part of the 1.1.0 release, and no public package directory is published on this site.</p> </div>  <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\">Previous: Structs and Enums</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/standard-library\">Next: Standard Library</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_standard_library() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"active\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Standard Library</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">This reference lists the built-ins and the <code>std</code> modules of the self-hosted RUDRA 1.1.0 release. Every function named here was compiled and run with the shipped compiler. The release includes 68 modules; this page covers the core ones.</p> <h2>Core built-ins</h2> <table> <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td><code>print(s)</code></td><td>Print a <code>str</code>; adds no newline, so append <code>\"\\n\"</code> yourself</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>to_str(n)</code></td><td>Convert an int to a string</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>str_len(s)</code></td><td>Length of a string</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>substr(s, start, length)</code></td><td>Part of a string</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>str_byte(s, i)</code></td><td>Byte value at an index</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>str_eq(a, b)</code></td><td>String equality</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>list_new()</code></td><td>Create a <code>list[int]</code> (capped at 512 elements)</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>push(l, v)</code></td><td>Append to a list</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>get(l, i)</code></td><td>Read element <code>i</code>; out of range aborts with exit 134</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>set(l, i, v)</code></td><td>Overwrite element <code>i</code></td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>list_len(l)</code></td><td>Number of elements</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <h2>Importing std modules</h2> <p>Add <code>import \"module\";</code> at the top of a file. <code>ruxc</code> resolves imports only from the source file's own directory, so compile programs that import std modules with <code>rudra file.rux output</code>, which copies the std library next to a temporary copy of your source first.</p> <h2>Common modules</h2> <table> <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td><code>math: abs_i, clamp, is_even, is_odd, gcd</code></td><td>Integer helpers</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>fmath: abs_f, min_f, max_f, floor_f, ceil_f, round_f, pow_i, sqrt_f, fmt_f64</code></td><td>Float helpers</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>strings: is_empty, is_blank, has, wrap</code></td><td>String helpers</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>list: sum_list, max_in_list, count_of</code></td><td>List helpers</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>sort: sort_asc, is_sorted</code></td><td>Sort a <code>list[int]</code></td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>convert: int_to_str, str_to_int, bool_to_str</code></td><td>Conversions</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>map: map_new, map_set, map_get, map_has, map_len</code></td><td>String key/value map</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> <tr><td><code>time: unix_now, mono_now, elapsed_secs</code></td><td>Clock access</td><td><span class=\"badge badge-verified\">VERIFIED</span></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>import &quot;math&quot;;\nimport &quot;strings&quot;;\nimport &quot;sort&quot;;\nimport &quot;list&quot;;\nimport &quot;convert&quot;;\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    let xs = list_new();\n    push(xs, 5);\n    push(xs, 2);\n    push(xs, 9);\n    let ys = sort_asc(xs);\n    print(to_str(get(ys, 0)) + &quot; &quot; + to_str(get(ys, 2)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(sum_list(xs)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(max_in_list(xs)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(gcd(12, 18)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(clamp(15, 0, 10)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(str_to_int(&quot;42&quot;) + 1) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    if has(&quot;hello world&quot;, &quot;world&quot;) {\n        print(&quot;found\\n&quot;);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div> <p>Output:</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>2 9\n16\n9\n6\n10\n43\nfound</code></pre> </div> <div class=\"callout callout-warn\"> <h4>Not built-ins</h4> <p>There are no <code>spawn</code>, <code>mutex_new</code>, <code>chan_new</code>, <code>http_get</code> or <code>json_get</code> built-ins, no built-in <code>len</code>, and no built-in Option/Result or <code>?</code>. Modules named <code>thread</code>, <code>chan</code>, <code>net</code>, <code>http</code>, <code>json</code> and <code>tls13*</code> exist in the std directory with documented limits, but they are library code, not language built-ins, and are not documented as verified here. TLS transport is not wired into a live authenticated HTTPS client.</p> </div>   <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/modules\">Previous: Modules and Projects</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/compiler\">Next: Compiler Guide</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_structs_and_enums() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"active\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Structs and Enums</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">RUDRA has structs for grouped data and enums for a fixed set of named values.</p> <h2>Structs</h2> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>struct Point { x: int, y: int }\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    let p = Point { x: 3, y: 4 };\n    print(to_str(p.x) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(p.y) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Enums</h2> <p>Variants are written with a dot: <code>Color.Green</code>. Put a <code>return</code> after a <code>match</code> that returns from its arms.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }\nfn name(c: Color) -&gt; str {\n    match c {\n        Color.Red =&gt; { return &quot;red&quot;; }\n        Color.Green =&gt; { return &quot;green&quot;; }\n        Color.Blue =&gt; { return &quot;blue&quot;; }\n    }\n    return &quot;&quot;;\n}\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(name(Color.Green) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <p>This program prints <code>green</code>.</p> <h2>Enums with payloads</h2> <p>Variants can carry a value, which a <code>match</code> arm binds to a name. This program prints <code>75</code> and then <code>0</code>.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>enum Shape { Circle(int), Rect(int), Empty }\nfn area(s: Shape) -&gt; int {\n    match s {\n        Shape.Circle(r) =&gt; { return 3 * r * r; }\n        Shape.Rect(w) =&gt; { return w * w; }\n        Shape.Empty =&gt; { return 0; }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\nfn main() -&gt; int {\n    print(to_str(area(Shape.Circle(5))) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(area(Shape.Empty)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div> <div class=\"callout callout-info\"> <h4>Pattern matching</h4> <p>A <code>match</code> over an enum is checked for exhaustiveness: every variant must be handled, or a wildcard pattern must cover the rest.</p> </div>  <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/control-flow\">Previous: Control Flow</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/modules\">Next: Modules and Projects</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_troubleshooting() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"active\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Troubleshooting</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">Solutions to common issues when installing and using the toolchain. Every message below was produced by the shipped 1.1.0 compiler.</p> <h2>ruxc: command not found</h2> <p>The install directory is not on your PATH. The Linux installer places binaries in <code>~/.local/bin</code> by default. Add it to your PATH and open a new terminal. There is no <code>rudra version</code> command in this release; to check the install, compile and run <code>hello.rux</code>.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"sh\"><code>export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\nruxc share/rudra/examples/hello.rux hello &amp;&amp; ./hello</code></pre> </div> <h2>My program compiles but prints nothing</h2> <p>Top-level statements are what run. A file containing only <code>fn main() {...}</code> prints nothing. End the file with <code>return main();</code>, and have <code>main</code> declared as <code>fn main() -&gt; int</code> returning a value; that value becomes the exit status.</p> <h2>ERROR import module not found</h2> <p><code>ruxc</code> resolves <code>import \"name\";</code> only from the directory of the source file. For std modules, compile with <code>rudra file.rux output</code>, which copies the std library next to a temporary copy of your source. For your own modules, keep the file beside the source.</p> <h2>TYPE print expects a string argument</h2> <p><code>print</code> takes only a <code>str</code>. Convert numbers first: <code>print(to_str(n) + \"\\n\");</code>.</p> <h2>CODEGEN unknown function</h2> <p>You called a function that does not exist, such as the built-in <code>len</code> (use <code>str_len</code> for strings and <code>list_len</code> for lists) or an unimported std function. Check the spelling and add the needed <code>import</code>.</p> <h2>ERROR non-exhaustive match</h2> <p>A <code>match</code> over an enum must cover every variant, or end with a <code>_</code> wildcard arm. Add the missing variants.</p> <h2>Program exits with status 134</h2> <p>Division or modulo by zero, and out-of-range list access, abort the program with exit status 134. Lists are also capped at 512 elements.</p> <h2>rudra new, rudra run or rudra version is not recognised</h2> <p>These commands are not part of the 1.1.0 release. A newer driver with <code>build</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>check</code> and <code>version</code> exists in the source repository but has not been released. Use <code>ruxc file.rux output</code> or <code>rudra file.rux output</code>, then run <code>./output</code>.</p> <h2>Checksum does not match</h2> <p>Re-download the asset and verify again. Do not install an archive whose SHA256 does not match the value on the <a href=\"/downloads/\">Downloads</a> page.</p>  <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/examples\">Previous: Examples</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/faq\">Next: FAQ</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_docs_variables_and_types() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\">  <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <div class=\"docs-layout\"> <aside class=\"docs-side\" aria-label=\"Documentation navigation\"> <div> <h4>Introduction</h4> <a href=\"/docs/getting-started\" class=\"\">Getting Started</a><a href=\"/docs/installation\" class=\"\">Installation</a> </div><div> <h4>Language</h4> <a href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\" class=\"\">Language Fundamentals</a><a href=\"/docs/variables-and-types\" class=\"active\">Variables and Types</a><a href=\"/docs/functions\" class=\"\">Functions</a><a href=\"/docs/control-flow\" class=\"\">Control Flow</a><a href=\"/docs/structs-and-enums\" class=\"\">Structs and Enums</a><a href=\"/docs/modules\" class=\"\">Modules and Projects</a> </div><div> <h4>Reference</h4> <a href=\"/docs/standard-library\" class=\"\">Standard Library</a><a href=\"/docs/compiler\" class=\"\">Compiler Guide</a><a href=\"/docs/examples\" class=\"\">Examples</a> </div><div> <h4>Help</h4> <a href=\"/docs/troubleshooting\" class=\"\">Troubleshooting</a><a href=\"/docs/faq\" class=\"\">FAQ</a> </div> </aside> <article class=\"docs-main\"> <h1>Variables and Types</h1>  <p class=\"lead\">RUDRA is statically typed. Bindings are immutable by default.</p> <h2>Bindings</h2> <p>Use <code>let</code> for an immutable binding and <code>let mut</code> for a mutable one.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn main() -&gt; int {\n    let name = &quot;RUDRA&quot;;\n    let mut count = 3;\n    count = count + 1;\n    print(name + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(count) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Type annotations</h2> <p>You can annotate a binding with its type. <code>print</code> accepts only a <code>str</code> and adds no newline, so convert numbers with <code>to_str</code>.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn main() -&gt; int {\n    let title: str = &quot;Systems language&quot;;\n    let answer: int = 42;\n    let ratio: float = 3.5;\n    let ok: bool = true;\n    print(title + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(answer) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    if ok {\n        print(&quot;ok\\n&quot;);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Core types</h2> <table> <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>Integer</td><td><code>42</code></td></tr> <tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>Floating-point number</td><td><code>3.14</code></td></tr> <tr><td><code>bool</code></td><td>Boolean</td><td><code>true</code>, <code>false</code></td></tr> <tr><td><code>str</code></td><td>String</td><td><code>\"text\"</code></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <h2>Lists</h2> <p>RUDRA has no fixed-size array literals. Use <code>list[int]</code>: create it with <code>list_new()</code>, add with <code>push</code>, read with <code>get</code> (indexed from zero) and count with <code>list_len</code>. Lists are capped at 512 elements, and an out-of-range access aborts the program (exit 134).</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn main() -&gt; int {\n    let nums = list_new();\n    push(nums, 10);\n    push(nums, 20);\n    push(nums, 30);\n    print(to_str(get(nums, 0)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(get(nums, 2)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(list_len(nums)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>  <h2>Strings</h2> <p>Strings concatenate with <code>+</code>; <code>str_len</code> returns a string length and <code>substr(s, start, length)</code> extracts part of a string.</p> <div class=\"codeblock-wrap\" style=\"position:relative\">  <pre class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"rux\"><code>fn main() -&gt; int {\n    let greeting = &quot;Namaste, &quot; + &quot;RUDRA&quot;;\n    print(greeting + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(to_str(str_len(greeting)) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    print(substr(greeting, 0, 7) + &quot;\\n&quot;);\n    return 0;\n}\nreturn main();</code></pre> </div>   <nav class=\"docs-nav-fb\" aria-label=\"Pagination\"> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/language-fundamentals\">Previous: Language Fundamentals</a> <a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/docs/functions\">Next: Functions</a> </nav> </article> </div> </div> </section>  </main>";
}
fn page_downloads() -> str {
    return "<main id=\"main\"> <section class=\"section\"> <div class=\"container\"> <p class=\"eyebrow\">Downloads</p> <h1>Verified releases</h1> <p class=\"lead\">Every asset below is attached to the official GitHub release. Each checksum was checked against the downloaded file.</p> <div class=\"callout callout-info\" style=\"margin-top:1.5rem\"> <h4>About the binaries</h4> <p>RUDRA 1.1.0 is built with the self-hosted compiler <code>ruxc</code>, which is written in RUDRA. The compiler is a static Linux x86-64 ELF executable with no runtime dependencies. Linux x86-64 is the only supported target.</p> </div> <h2 style=\"margin-top:2.2rem\">Current release: 1.1.0</h2> <div class=\"grid\" style=\"margin-top:1rem\"> <div class=\"card\"> <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem\"> <h3 style=\"margin:0\">Linux &middot; x86_64 &middot; archive</h3> <span class=\"badge badge-verified\">v1.1.0</span> </div> <p style=\"font-size:0.92rem;margin:0.5rem 0\"><code>rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz</code> &middot; 242,197 bytes &middot; static ELF</p> <p style=\"font-size:0.8rem;color:var(--text-faint);word-break:break-all;margin:0.3rem 0\">SHA256: 13c31b6cadb74404812783e369fde08032dd671085c384c279e900073a7043b2</p> <a class=\"btn btn-primary\" href=\"https://github.com/rakshanex/rudra/releases/download/v1.1.0/rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"margin-top:0.6rem\">Download</a> </div> <div class=\"card\"> <div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem\"> <h3 style=\"margin:0\">Debian / Ubuntu &middot; amd64</h3> <span class=\"badge badge-verified\">v1.1.0</span> </div> <p style=\"font-size:0.92rem;margin:0.5rem 0\"><code>rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-amd64.deb</code> &middot; 170,308 bytes</p> <p style=\"font-size:0.8rem;color:var(--text-faint);word-break:break-all;margin:0.3rem 0\">SHA256: 902a3ea0e95fa7bd85e7a153d235de648a09981f5d85d4f5fc3accb7147f88f6</p> <a class=\"btn btn-primary\" href=\"https://github.com/rakshanex/rudra/releases/download/v1.1.0/rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-amd64.deb\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"margin-top:0.6rem\">Download</a> </div> </div> <h2 style=\"margin-top:2.5rem\">Verify your download</h2> <pre class=\"codeblock\"><code>sha256sum rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz\n# expected: 13c31b6cadb74404812783e369fde08032dd671085c384c279e900073a7043b2</code></pre> <h2 style=\"margin-top:2rem\">Install (Linux)</h2> <pre class=\"codeblock\"><code>tar -xzf rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64.tar.gz\ncd rudra-1.1.0-selfhost-linux-x86_64 &amp;&amp; ./install.sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\nrudra share/rudra/examples/hello.rux hello &amp;&amp; ./hello</code></pre> <h2 style=\"margin-top:2.5rem\">Older releases</h2> <p>Versions 1.0.0 and 1.0.1 were built with the earlier Rust-based toolchain. They included macOS and Windows installers. Those builds are still on GitHub, but they are not the current self-hosted compiler.</p> <p style=\"margin-top:1rem\">All releases: <a href=\"https://github.com/rakshanex/rudra/releases\" rel=\"noopener\">https://github.com/rakshanex/rudra/releases</a></p> </div> </section> </main>";
}
